{
  id:'npqb-nr-316', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'Which of the following is the most common cause of lower back pain (LBP)?',
  options:['Lumbar disc herniation', 'Mechanical back pain', 'Osteoporotic fracture', 'Spinal tumor'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Mechanical back pain is the correct answer. In the lecture on back pain, lower back pain is characterized as a broad clinical term rather than a specific single disease entity, and it represents one of the most common reasons for neurological and neurosurgical consultation. The lecture explicitly notes that among the wide variety of potential etiologies, the most common cause of lower back pain is mechanical back pain, which typically arises from acute or repetitive muscle, tendon, or ligamentous strain. Furthermore, in up to 85% of presenting individuals, no specific anatomical or structural cause can be isolated, making simple mechanical strain the overwhelmingly predominant diagnostic category encountered in primary care settings. Work-up in these uncomplicated cases focuses primarily on screening for red flags to rule out serious underlying pathology, without requiring routine laboratory work or neuroimaging.\n\nThe distractor options represent less common or specific secondary pathological causes of lower back pain. Lumbar disc herniation (Option A) occurs when the nucleus pulposus herniates through the annulus fibrosus; while it is a major cause of nerve root compromise and sciatica (most commonly affecting L5 and S1 roots), it accounts for a much smaller fraction of overall LBP cases compared to non-specific mechanical pain. Osteoporotic fracture (Option C) is a structural bony cause that often presents as acute pain following minor trauma in vulnerable patients (not taken from the course material) and serves as a red flag requiring targeted diagnostic evaluation. Spinal tumor (Option D) is a neoplastic etiology that constitutes a critical red flag (suggested by constant, progressive pain worse at night or supine, or a prior cancer history), making it a rare and dangerous cause rather than a common broad cause. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify mechanical back pain as the most common cause of lower back pain.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q316, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-317', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A 45-year-old male presents with acute low back pain after lifting a heavy object. He denies fever, weight loss, or any history of cancer. The pain is localized and does not radiate down the legs. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Herniated disc', 'Mechanical low back pain', 'Spinal metastasis', 'Cauda equina syndrome'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Mechanical low back pain is the correct diagnosis. According to the lecture on lower back pain, acute low back pain is defined clinically as pain located below the costal margins and above the inferior gluteal folds without serious underlying pathology. It is a very common presenting illness in the primary care setting that frequently recurs and resolves spontaneously regardless of the specific treatment administered. Etiologically, acute back pain commonly occurs following physical exertion, such as a single major trauma like lifting a heavy weight or repetitive microtrauma. In this vignette, the 45-year-old patient developed acute low back pain immediately after lifting a heavy object, matching the typical mechanical precipitating event. Furthermore, his presentation consists purely of localized back pain without radiation into the lower extremities, which fits the isolated "back syndrome" pattern described in the course material rather than a radicular "leg syndrome" or combined "back and leg syndrome."\n\nA central principle in managing acute low back pain is screening for clinical red flags to rule out serious underlying structural, infectious, or neoplastic conditions. The lecture explicitly outlines key red flags, including age under 20 or over 50 with initial presentation, trauma, constant and progressive pain, pain worse at night or when supine, previous history of cancer, corticosteroid or intravenous drug use, immunosuppression, fever, unexplained weight loss, and neurological deficits. The vignette specifically notes that the patient denies fever, weight loss, or any previous cancer history, and exhibits no neurological signs or leg radiation. In the absence of these red flags, the clinical work-up establishes simple, uncomplicated mechanical low back pain. For such patients between the ages of 20 and 50, the lecture guidelines explicitly advise against ordering routine laboratory investigations or diagnostic imaging (such as plain X-rays or MRI), recommending instead conservative management aimed at keeping the patient active.\n\nThe distractor options represent distinct pathological entities that are excluded by the patient\'s presentation and absence of red flags:\n- Herniated disc occurs when the soft nucleus pulposus herniates through a tear in the annulus fibrosus. It classically presents with radicular symptoms, such as leg pain (sciatica) radiating down the L5 or S1 nerve root distribution, often accompanied by motor weakness, sensory changes, or reflex alterations (decreased patellar or ankle jerks). The complete absence of leg pain or radicular neurological signs in this patient makes a herniated disc unlikely.\n- Spinal metastasis is a malignant secondary cause of back pain. In the lecture\'s red flag screening framework, malignancy is heralded by a previous history of cancer, unexplained weight loss, fever, or constant back pain that progressively worsens and intensifies at night or in the supine position. The patient\'s explicit denial of cancer history, weight loss, and systemic symptoms rules out metastatic disease.\n- Cauda equina syndrome is a neurological emergency caused by a large central lumbar disc herniation compressing the nerve roots of the cauda equina. It produces a severe, characteristic clinical picture including low back pain, bilateral sciatica, flail foot or leg weakness, saddle-shaped hypoesthesia, and lower motor neuron sphincteric disturbance (such as urinary retention with overflow incontinence and chronic constipation). None of these alarming bilateral or sphincteric features are present. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize acute localized mechanical low back pain following heavy lifting without red flags.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q317, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-318', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'A 60-year-old male with a history of neck pain for the last several months presents with new-onset arm pain radiating down the left arm. The pain is worsened by neck movement and relieved by arm abduction. He has numbness and tingling along the left C6 dermatome. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cervical spondylosis', 'Cervical disc prolapses (C5-C6 level)', 'Brachial plexus injury', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Cervical disc prolapse (C5-C6 level) is the correct diagnosis. According to the course material on cervical pain, a cervical disc prolapse occurs when the soft nucleus pulposus herniates through a tear in the annulus fibrosus, most frequently affecting the middle-aged population between 30 and 50 years. The clinical presentation depends on the direction of the herniation: if the prolapse is central, it causes spinal cord compression leading to myelopathy, whereas a posterolateral prolapse causes nerve root compression leading to radiculopathy. The clinical features of cervical radiculopathy detailed in the lecture include neck pain and stiffness, combined with brachialgia, which is pain radiating down to the arm and hand. This radiating pain is classically exacerbated by neck motion—specifically neck extension—and is characteristically relieved by arm abduction, known as the arm abduction test. Furthermore, patients experience paresthesia and numbness along the dermatome corresponding to the compressed nerve root, alongside motor weakness in long-standing compression and signs such as loss of cervical lordosis, local tenderness, limitation of spinal movement, and paravertebral muscle spasm.\n\nIn this patient, the specific combination of left arm pain radiating down the arm, exacerbation with neck movement, relief upon arm abduction, and sensory loss specifically confined to the left C6 dermatome directly points to compromise of the C6 nerve root. The lecture explicitly states that the C6 nerve root is most commonly affected in a C5-C6 disc herniation. Therefore, the anatomical level and root involvement match the C5-C6 cervical disc prolapse diagnosis.\n\nThe distractor options are incorrect for specific mechanical and clinical reasons:\n- Cervical spondylosis is a degenerative disorder of the cervical spine that can compress the spinal cord or nerve roots. While it is the most common cause of neck pain overall and most commonly localizes to the C5-C6 and C6-C7 levels, its classic presentation features waking up in the morning with a painful stiff neck and a diffuse non-pulsatile headache that resolves in a few hours, along with wasting and weakness of the small muscles of the hands (particularly weakness of abduction of the little finger) and restricted neck movement. The acute posterolateral radicular pain radiating down the arm with specific C6 dermatomal numbness and relief with arm abduction distinguishes a soft disc herniation from broad degenerative spondylosis.\n- Brachial plexus injury involves trauma or pathology to the brachial plexus rather than single root compression from a cervical disc herniation, and its specific clinical features and distribution are not taken from the course material.\n- Thoracic outlet syndrome involves compression of neurovascular structures in the thoracic outlet, producing distinct upper extremity symptoms that do not correspond to the lecture\'s cervical disc prolapse findings or the arm abduction test. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize C5-C6 cervical disc prolapse from C6 radicular arm pain and relief with arm abduction.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q318, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-319', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'A 50-year-old patient complains of chronic neck pain and stiffness with headaches. Examination, there is loss of cervical lordosis and tenderness at the C5-C6 level with a weakness in abduction of the little finger. Which condition is most likely?',
  options:['Cervical spondylosis', 'Cervical disc prolapses', 'Radiculopathy due to a herniated disc'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Cervical spondylosis is the correct diagnosis. According to the lecture on cervical pain, cervical spondylosis is defined as a degenerative disorder of the cervical spine which can compress the spinal cord or nerve roots. It is taught as the most common overall cause of neck pain and brachialgia in clinical practice. The clinical presentation of cervical spondylosis has several hallmark features: patients frequently awake in the morning with a painful, stiff neck accompanied by a diffuse, non-pulsatile headache that typically resolves within a few hours. The degenerative lesions and focal clinical signs of cervical spondylosis most commonly localize to the C5-C6 and C6-C7 vertebral levels. Furthermore, the lecture explicitly highlights that wasting and weakness of the small muscles of the hands—particularly weakness of abduction of the little finger—is frequently seen in cervical spondylosis due to chronic root or cord involvement. On physical examination, restricted neck movement and loss of normal cervical lordosis are always present in significant cases of cervical spondylosis. Diagnostic evaluation using plain X-rays of the cervical spine typically reveals loss of normal cervical lordosis, narrowing of the disc space, and osteophytes, whereas MRI provides the best overall diagnostic visualization. In patients with severe cord compression, additional manifestations such as bladder dysfunction, long-tract signs, or spastic quadriparesis may develop.\n\nIn this vignette, the 50-year-old patient\'s presentation of chronic neck pain and stiffness, headaches, loss of cervical lordosis, C5-C6 tenderness, and characteristic little finger abduction weakness perfectly match the course profile of cervical spondylosis.\n\nThe distractor options are incorrect for specific clinical and pathological reasons:\n- Cervical disc prolapses involve the acute herniation of the soft nucleus pulposus through a tear in the annulus fibrosus, typically occurring in middle-aged individuals (30-50 years). They classically present with acute posterolateral radiculopathy or brachialgia that is exacerbated by neck extension and specifically relieved by arm abduction, accompanied by dermatomal paresthesia and numbness. They do not present with the chronic spondylotic morning headache pattern, diffuse stiffness, or the specific hand intrinsic muscle wasting described in this patient.\n- Radiculopathy due to a herniated disc represents a focal nerve root compression syndrome resulting from an acute disc herniation rather than the broad, multi-level degenerative spinal disease characterized by restricted movement, loss of lordosis, and intrinsic hand muscle weakness. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate chronic neck pain, headaches, loss of lordosis, and little finger weakness with cervical spondylosis.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q319, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-320', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A 40-year-old male presents with chronic LBP. He has no history of trauma, and the pain is localized to the lower back, without any radiation to the legs. He also reports feeling depressed and has difficulty concentrating due to the pain. He is working long hours. What is the most appropriate management?',
  options:['Opioid analgesics', 'Multidisciplinary approach for both physical and psychological aspects', 'MRI of the lumbar spine', 'Referral to a pain clinic for injections'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Multidisciplinary approach for both physical and psychological aspects is the correct management. According to the course material on chronic back pain, chronic low back pain is defined as low back pain of unspecified pathology that persists longer than 3 months. The lecture emphasizes that the evolution of chronic low back pain is complex, involving intricate physiological, psychological, and psychosocial influences. Specifically, depression is frequently associated with chronic back pain, whereas malingering is uncommon. Consequently, the work-up for chronic back pain must include a thorough screening for yellow flags—special scales and scores utilized to assess social, psychological, and occupational disabilities that indicate a high risk for chronic disability. In this clinical vignette, the 40-year-old male presents with localized chronic low back pain lasting over three months without red flags, but he explicitly exhibits significant psychosocial and psychological manifestations, including feeling depressed, experiencing difficulty concentrating due to the pain, and working long hours. Per the lecture guidelines, in the absence of red flags, chronic back pain must be treated in a multi-modal but conservative fashion where early return to work is a priority and the patient\'s ideas about pain and other yellow flags are addressed with appropriate sensitivity, aiming to keep the patient active as soon as possible. A multidisciplinary approach integrating physical management with psychological and psychosocial support directly addresses these yellow flags and the associated depression, fulfilling the therapeutic requirements outlined in the course material.\n\nThe distractor management options are inappropriate or insufficient for this clinical presentation:\n- Opioid analgesics are not supported as primary or routine management for chronic mechanical low back pain with psychosocial complexities in the course material. Conservative treatment modalities emphasized in the lecture focus on physical therapy, activity modification, NSAIDs, and functional restoration rather than potent narcotic escalation.\n- MRI of the lumbar spine is not indicated as a primary intervention for localized chronic back pain. The lecture notes that while AP and lateral lumbosacral X-rays may be appropriate in chronic low back pain, and MRI may be indicated if pain radiates below the knees, ordering advanced imaging for a patient with localized chronic back pain and no red flags or radicular symptoms does not alter the conservative multi-modal management strategy.\n- Referral to a pain clinic for injections is an invasive interventional procedure that is not taught as the primary first-line management for uncomplicated chronic low back pain with psychological yellow flags. The course material mandates conservative, multi-modal care addressing psychological influences and promoting early return to activity. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Choose a multidisciplinary conservative management approach for chronic low back pain with psychological yellow flags.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q320, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-321', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'What is the most common cause of sciatica?',
  options:['Lumbar dacs prolapse', 'Diabetic ischemia of the nerve', 'Sciatic nerve entrapment'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Lumbar disc prolapse is the correct answer. According to the course material, sciatica describes pain referred down the course of the sciatic nerve from the back to the buttock, and down the back of the leg to the foot. The lecture explicitly details that the most common cause of sciatica arises from compromise of the L5 and S1 spinal nerve roots, which most frequently occurs due to lumbar disc prolapse (where the soft nucleus pulposus herniates through a tear in the annulus fibrosus). \n\nThe distractor causes of leg and nerve pain are less common or secondary mechanisms:\n- Diabetic ischemia of the nerve and roots is explicitly listed in the lecture as an etiology of sciatica, but it represents a systemic vascular and metabolic cause rather than the primary mechanical structural cause (disc prolapse) responsible for the vast majority of clinical presentations.\n- Sciatic nerve entrapment is not highlighted in the lecture as the primary or most common mechanism of spinal sciatica; the course material attributes sciatica predominantly to root compromise from lumbar disc herniation, diabetic ischemia, and neoplastic involvement. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify lumbar disc prolapse as the most common cause of sciatica.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q321, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-322', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'The MRI cervical spine of patient showed C5 root compression, what is the most common sine of numbness in that patient?',
  options:['Medial aspect of arm', 'Medial aspect of forearm', 'lateral aspect of the left arm', 'Lateral aspect of forearm'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'The lateral aspect of the left arm is the correct answer. The lecture on cervical pain discusses cervical disc prolapse and cervical spondylosis, noting that posterolateral disc herniations cause nerve root compression (radiculopathy) leading to paresthesia and numbness along the dermatome corresponding to the affected nerve root, such as the C5 root. While L11 establishes that cervical radiculopathy causes dermatomal sensory changes and lists C5 as a compromised root in upper cervical pathology, the specific correlation that C5 root compression maps to the lateral aspect of the arm is general neuroanatomy [not taken from the course material].\n\nThe distractor sensory localizations correspond to different nerve root levels:\n- Medial aspect of the arm corresponds to lower cervical and T1 dermatomal distributions [not taken from the course material], not C5 root compression.\n- Medial aspect of the forearm corresponds to C8 or T1 nerve root sensory distributions [not taken from the course material], rather than C5.\n- Lateral aspect of the forearm corresponds to the C6 dermatome [not taken from the course material] (as seen in C5-C6 disc prolapse), not the C5 root level. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate C5 cervical root compression with sensory loss on the lateral aspect of the arm.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q322, book p.61'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-323', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'A 55-year-old woman complains of chronic neck pain that radiates to her right shoulder and arm. The pain has been ongoing for the past 6 months and is aggravated by neck movement, particularly neck extension. On examination, she has weakness of the right triceps and diminished sensation over the middle finger. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Cervical spondylosis with radiculopathy (C6-C7)', 'Cervical disc prolapse at C5-C6', 'Cervical myelopathy', 'Thoracic outlet syndrome'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Cervical spondylosis with radiculopathy (C6-C7) is the correct diagnosis. According to the course material on cervical pain, cervical spondylosis is defined as a degenerative disorder of the cervical spine which can compress the spinal cord or nerve roots, and it is taught as the most common cause of neck pain and brachialgia. The lecture notes that degenerative lesions in cervical spondylosis are most commonly located at the C5-C6 and C6-C7 vertebral levels, and specifically states that the C7 nerve root is most commonly affected in C6-C7 pathology. In this clinical vignette, the 55-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of chronic neck pain radiating to her right shoulder and arm (brachialgia) that is aggravated by neck movement, particularly neck extension—classic features of cervical radiculopathy described in the course material. On examination, she exhibits weakness of the right triceps and diminished sensation over the middle finger. While the lecture details that radiculopathy causes motor weakness and dermatomal sensory changes in the distribution of the compressed root, the specific neuroanatomical correlation mapping triceps weakness and middle finger sensory loss to the C7 myotome and dermatome is general neuroanatomy [not taken from the course material]. Combining these findings with the chronic degenerative presentation and C6-C7 localization yields cervical spondylosis with C6-C7 radiculopathy.\n\nThe distractor options are incorrect for specific clinical, anatomical, and pathological reasons:\n- Cervical disc prolapse at C5-C6 involves acute or subacute herniation of the nucleus pulposus through the annulus fibrosus in middle-aged individuals, whereas this patient has a 6-month chronic degenerative presentation consistent with spondylosis. Anatomically, a C5-C6 disc prolapse primarily compresses the C6 nerve root (classically causing lateral forearm numbness and biceps weakness), rather than the C7 root involvement (triceps and middle finger) seen in C6-C7 pathology.\n- Cervical myelopathy results from central spinal cord compression (due to central disc prolapse or severe spondylosis) rather than posterolateral root compression. The lecture defines the clinical picture of myelopathy by spinal cord signs, including spastic quadriparesis, hyperreflexia below the level of compression, clumsiness, ataxic gait disturbances, and sphincter dysfunction, which are entirely distinct from the isolated single-root radicular weakness and sensory loss presented by this patient.\n- Thoracic outlet syndrome is a peripheral neurovascular compression syndrome affecting the brachial plexus or subclavian vessels at the superior thoracic aperture [not taken from the course material]. It is not taught in the course material as a neurological cause of cervical spine spondylosis or C7 nerve root radiculopathy. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize C6-C7 cervical spondylosis with radiculopathy from triceps weakness and middle finger sensory loss.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q323, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-324', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'The most common site for cervical disc herniation is:',
  options:['C4-C5', 'C5-C6', 'C6-C7', 'C7-T1'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'C5-C6 is the correct answer. The lecture on cervical disc prolapse explicitly states that the most common affected levels are C5-C6 and C6-C7, with C5-C6 being the most frequent site of herniation and the primary anatomical level for C6 nerve root affection. Mechanically, the mid-cervical spine segments (specifically C5-C6) bear the highest biomechanical axial load, range of motion, and mechanical stress in the cervical column [not taken from the course material], making the annulus fibrosus at this level exceptionally vulnerable to tears and subsequent nucleus pulposus herniation under axial loading or trauma.\n\nC4-C5 is less frequently affected, and herniation at this level typically compromises the C5 nerve root rather than the more common C6 or C7 roots. C6-C7 is a very common site of cervical disc prolapse (affecting the C7 nerve root), but C5-C6 is cited in the lecture as the primary most frequent level of involvement. C7-T1 is an uncommon site for cervical disc herniation compared to the mid-cervical levels. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify C5-C6 as the most common site for cervical disc herniation.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q324, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-325', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'Which of the following is the best diagnostic technique for cervical disc herniation?',
  options:['Plain X-ray', 'MRI cervical spine', 'CT cervical spine', 'Ultrasound cervical spine'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'MRI cervical spine is the correct answer. The lecture investigations section for cervical spine disorders explicitly designates MRI cervical spine as the best diagnostic technique. MRI provides superior soft-tissue contrast resolution and high-resolution imaging of the intervertebral discs, exiting nerve roots, dorsal and ventral nerve roots, and the spinal cord. This allows clinicians to precisely visualize soft disc prolapse, posterolateral root compression resulting in radiculopathy, and central cord compression leading to myelopathy.\n\nPlain X-ray is useful for identifying radiographic bony abnormalities such as loss of normal cervical lordosis, intervertebral disc space narrowing, osteophyte formation, and cervical subluxation, but it lacks the soft-tissue resolution required to directly visualize a herniated intervertebral disc or neural compression. CT cervical spine is more useful for detailed bony anatomy, detecting osteophytes, diagnosing congenital cervical stenosis, and evaluating ossified posterior longitudinal ligament (OPLL), but it is inferior to MRI for soft tissue disc pathology. Ultrasound cervical spine is not a recognized or validated primary diagnostic tool for evaluating cervical disc herniation in the course material. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify MRI cervical spine as the best diagnostic technique for cervical disc herniation.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q325, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-326', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'Which of the following findings on a plain X-ray of the cervical spine would raise suspicion for cervical disc herniation?',
  options:['Loss of normal cervical lordosis', 'Increased cervical lordosis', 'Widening of the disc space', 'Decreased osteophyte formation'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'Loss of normal cervical lordosis is the correct answer. The lecture investigations section for plain X-ray cervical spine lists loss of normal cervical lordosis, along with disc space narrowing, osteophytes, and cervical subluxation, as characteristic findings in cervical disc disease and spondylosis. Mechanically, loss of the normal cervical lordotic curve is caused by painful reflex spasm of the paravertebral neck muscles, an involuntary protective mechanism activated to stabilize, guard, and immobilize the painful cervical segments affected by disc herniation, nerve root irritation, or degenerative strain.\n\nIncreased cervical lordosis is abnormal and not a characteristic radiographic feature associated with cervical disc herniation or acute muscle spasm. Widening of the disc space is incorrect because cervical disc degeneration and prolapse result in structural degradation and narrowing of the intervertebral disc space due to loss of disc height. Decreased osteophyte formation is incorrect because chronic degenerative pathology and spondylosis lead to progressive osteophyte development along the vertebral endplates rather than a reduction in osteophytes. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify loss of normal cervical lordosis as an X-ray finding in cervical disc herniation.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q326, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-327', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-neckpain',
  stem:'A 50-year-old patient with cervical disc herniation continues to have severe brachialgia despite medical treatment. The most appropriate next step would be:',
  options:['MRI cervical spine', 'Surgery for decompression', 'A second round of physical therapy', 'Bed rest and avoidance of lifting'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Surgery for decompression is the correct answer. The lecture on cervical pain and its management explicitly lists indications for surgical intervention as: brachialgia not responding to medical treatment, progressive neurological deficit due to root compression, and manifestations of spinal cord compression (myelopathy). This 50-year-old patient presented with cervical disc herniation and severe brachialgia (radiating arm pain) that has persisted despite medical treatment. Brachialgia and neck pain are very common clinical problems experienced by a significant proportion of the population. When brachialgia persists despite appropriate conservative management—which encompasses bed rest, activity modification, avoidance of heavy lifting, physical therapy, analgesics, NSAIDs, muscle relaxants, and neck collars—it indicates that the mechanical compression on the nerve root is severe, unremitting, and refractory to non-surgical modalities. Surgical decompression is specifically indicated in this clinical vignette to relieve the direct mechanical compression exerted by the herniated nucleus pulposus on the affected cervical spinal nerve root, thereby alleviating the disabling radiating pain and preventing permanent neurological deficits or irreversible nerve fiber damage.\n\nMRI cervical spine is a diagnostic tool utilized to confirm disc herniation and characterize neural compression, not a therapeutic management step. It is assumed that the patient has already undergone appropriate diagnostic imaging (such as an MRI) to establish the definitive diagnosis of cervical disc herniation before medical treatment was initiated and subsequently failed. Therefore, ordering another MRI is not the required next step in the clinical management pathway for a patient with an already established diagnosis who presents with symptoms that have proven refractory to conservative therapy. A second round of physical therapy is inappropriate because physical therapy is a cornerstone of conservative management, and the clinical vignette explicitly specifies that conservative management, including therapy, has already been attempted and failed over the course of treatment. Repeating physical therapy will not resolve the underlying structural mechanical nerve root compression. Bed rest and avoidance of lifting are conservative measures that were part of the initial failed conservative regimen and do not address the clinical requirement for definitive surgical intervention when severe, disabling symptoms fail to improve. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify persistent brachialgia unresponsive to medical treatment as an indication for surgical decompression.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q327, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-328', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A 70-year-old woman presents with acute back pain followed by weakness of dorsiflexion of her left foot. Where would you expect the associated sensory loss?',
  options:['Anterior thigh', 'Dorsum of foot', 'Perineum', 'Posterior calf', 'Sole of foot'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'The printed box says: Explanation: The features suggest an LS radiculopathy which would be associated with a loss of sensation in the dorsum of the foot and big toe. Dorsum of foot is the keyed answer. According to the course material on lower back pain and sciatica, lumbar disc prolapse occurs when the soft nucleus pulposus herniates through a tear in the annulus fibrosus, leading to mechanical compression of exiting nerve roots resulting in radiculopathy. The lecture explicitly states that the most common nerve roots affected in lumbar disc herniation are the S1 root in L5-S1 herniation and the L5 root in L4-L5 herniation, with L4 root affected to a lesser extent in L3-L4 herniation.\n\nWhen an intervertebral disc herniates posterolaterally at the L4-L5 level, it compresses the traversing L5 nerve root. Functionally, the L5 root supplies the motor innervation for ankle and foot dorsiflexion, notably via the tibialis anterior muscle and extensor hallucis longus. Consequently, compression or compromise of the L5 nerve root results in characteristic motor weakness of foot dorsiflexion. Sensory manifestations of L5 radiculopathy include paresthesia, numbness, and sensory loss along the dermatomal distribution of the L5 root. While L11 establishes that L5 radiculopathy causes dermatomal sensory changes in the lower limb and foot, the specific association mapping L5 sensory loss to the dorsum of the foot and big toe is reinforced by the printed box text [not taken from the course material]. Furthermore, L11 details that clinical examination of radiculopathy includes motor weakness in the muscle group supplied by the compressed root, dermatomal sensory changes, decreased reflexes, and a positive straight leg raising test.\n\nThe distractor options represent sensory distributions corresponding to different spinal nerve roots or anatomical regions not aligned with L5 radiculopathy and foot dorsiflexion weakness:\n- Anterior thigh sensory loss corresponds primarily to the L3 and L4 nerve root levels [not taken from the course material], which are evaluated in mid-lumbar pathology and tested clinically via the knee-jerk reflex rather than L5 dorsiflexion weakness.\n- Perineum sensory loss indicates involvement of the lower sacral roots or the characteristic saddle-shaped hypesthesia associated with cauda equina syndrome resulting from a large central lumbar disc herniation, rather than an isolated L5 monoradiculopathy.\n- Posterior calf sensory loss is classically associated with S1 nerve root compromise typically resulting from an L5-S1 disc herniation, which clinically presents with weakness of plantar flexion and diminished or absent ankle reflexes rather than foot dorsiflexion weakness.\n- Sole of foot sensory loss also corresponds to the S1 dermatome [not taken from the course material], reflecting S1 root radiculopathy rather than L5 involvement. Thus, among the provided choices, sensory loss on the dorsum of the foot correctly matches the L5 root lesion responsible for foot dorsiflexion weakness.',
  objective:'Associate foot dorsiflexion weakness with sensory loss on the dorsum of the foot.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q328, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-329', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A patient with sciatica has pain radiating down the back of the leg to the foot. Which of the following is most likely involved?',
  options:['L4-L5 nerve root', 'L5-S1 nerve root', 'S1-S2 nerve root', 'T12-L1 nerve root'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'L5-S1 nerve root is the keyed answer. According to the course material on lower back and lower limb pain, sciatica describes pain referred down the course of the sciatic nerve from the back into the buttock, down the back of the leg to the foot. The lecture explicitly details that the most common cause of sciatica arises from compromise of the L5 and S1 spinal nerve roots, resulting most frequently from lumbar disc prolapse. Specifically, the lecture states that the most common nerve root affected is the S1 nerve root in L5-S1 disc herniation, followed by the L5 nerve root in L4-L5 disc herniation and the L4 nerve root in L3-L4 herniation. Therefore, when sciatica presents with pain radiating down the posterior aspect of the leg to the foot, involvement of the L5-S1 level (specifically the S1 nerve root) is the most frequent anatomical source.\n\nThe distractor options represent different spinal levels or combinations that do not align with the primary sciatica root frequency taught in the course:\n- L4-L5 nerve root typically corresponds to L5 root compression associated with numbness in the outer thigh and foot and weakness of dorsiflexion, which represents the second most common site rather than the primary S1 posterior leg distribution.\n- S1-S2 nerve root and T12-L1 nerve root are not cited in the course material as the primary common anatomical levels responsible for mechanical sciatica; T12-L1 is thoracolumbar, and S1-S2 exceeds the standard single-root levels emphasized for common disc herniations. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate sciatica radiating down the back of the leg to the foot with the S1 nerve root.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q329, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-330', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'Which of the following clinical features is suggestive of cauda equina syndrome?',
  options:['Unilateral leg weakness and sensory loss', 'Urinary retention with overflow incontinence', 'Positive straight leg raising test', 'Leg pain aggravated by coughing and sneezing'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Urinary retention with overflow incontinence is the correct and keyed feature. According to the course material, cauda equina syndrome occurs due to a large central lumbar disc herniation compressing the cauda equina bundle, which constitutes a surgical emergency requiring early surgical intervention to prevent permanent neurological deficits. The lecture explicitly details the clinical symptoms of cauda equina syndrome, which include low back pain, bilateral sciatica, and lower motor neuron sphincteric disturbances—specifically urine retention with overflow incontinence and chronic constipation—alongside autonomic sexual dysfunction such as impotence in males, bilateral lower motor neuron motor weakness (flail foot), bilateral sensory hypesthesia, saddle-shaped hypesthesia due to sacral root affection, absent knee and ankle reflexes bilaterally, and positive bilateral straight leg raising tests.\n\nThe distractor clinical features represent different pathological entities rather than cauda equina syndrome:\n- Unilateral leg weakness and sensory loss are characteristic features of single-root monoradiculopathy or posterolateral lumbar disc prolapse, rather than the bilateral multi-root autonomic and sphincteric emergency of cauda equina syndrome.\n- Positive straight leg raising test and leg pain aggravated by coughing and sneezing are classical signs of mechanical nerve root tension, radiculopathy, or uncomplicated sciatica resulting from posterolateral disc herniation, rather than specific sphincteric cauda equina manifestations. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify urinary retention with overflow incontinence as a clinical feature of cauda equina syndrome.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q330, book p.62'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-331', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A 45-year-old patient with lumbar disc prolapse complains of pain radiating down the back of the leg, along with numbness in the outer thigh and foot. Which of the following findings is most consistent with this presentation?',
  options:['Decreased sensation along L5 dermatome, weakness of dorsiflexion', 'Decreased sensation along S1 dermatome, weakness of plantar flexion', 'Absent patellar reflexes', 'Positive Trendelenburg sign'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'The printed box says: Explanation:(L5 is commonly affected in disc prolapse at the L4-L5 level.) Decreased sensation along L5 dermatome, weakness of dorsiflexion is the keyed answer. The patient\'s clinical presentation—a 45-year-old with lumbar disc prolapse complaining of pain radiating down the back of the leg alongside numbness in the outer thigh and foot—points directly to L5 radiculopathy. According to the course material on lumbar disc prolapse and sciatica, disc herniation occurs when the soft nucleus pulposus herniates through a tear in the annulus fibrosus, leading to mechanical compression of nerve roots. The lecture explicitly states that the most common nerve root affected is the S1 nerve root in L5-S1 disc herniation, and the L5 root in L4-L5 disc herniation, with the L4 root affected to a lesser extent in L3-L4 disc herniation. As confirmed by the printed box text, L5 is specifically and commonly affected in disc prolapse at the L4-L5 vertebral level.\n\nAnatomically and clinically, posterolateral compression of the L5 nerve root resulting from an L4-L5 disc prolapse produces specific radicular findings. The course material details that signs of radiculopathy include motor weakness in the muscle group supplied by the compressed nerve root, sensory changes and dermatomal affection along the compressed root, decreased reflexes, and a positive straight leg raising test. Specifically, compression of the L5 nerve root leads to weakness of foot and toe dorsiflexion (such as tibialis anterior and extensor hallucis longus weakness) and sensory disturbances. While L11 teaches that L5 radiculopathy causes dermatomal sensory changes in the lower limb and foot, the specific association of outer thigh and foot numbness with the L5 dermatome is detailed in clinical practice [not taken from the course material]. Thus, decreased sensation along the L5 dermatome combined with weakness of dorsiflexion correctly matches the presentation of L4-L5 disc prolapse.\n\nThe distractor options represent clinical findings associated with different spinal root levels or pathology:\n- Decreased sensation along the S1 dermatome and weakness of plantar flexion correspond to S1 nerve root compromise resulting from an L5-S1 disc herniation, which differs from the L5 distribution of L4-L5 prolapse.\n- Absent patellar reflexes test the integrity of the mid-lumbar L3 and L4 nerve roots associated with femoral nerve and L3-L4 pathology [not taken from the course material], rather than the L5 root level affected in L4-L5 disc herniation.\n- Positive Trendelenburg sign indicates weakness of the hip abductors (gluteus medius and minimus), which are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve [not taken from the course material], and is classically associated with hip pathology or high lumbar lesions rather than isolated L5 radiculopathy from L4-L5 disc prolapse.',
  objective:'Associate outer leg and foot numbness and dorsiflexion weakness with L5 radiculopathy in L4-L5 disc prolapse.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q331, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-332', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A positive straight leg raising test (SLRT) is indicative of:',
  options:['Radiculopathy', 'Cauda equina syndrome', 'Sciatica', 'Both A and C'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'The printed key for this question is D — Both A and C, a compound and defective key that lists two correct-seeming options rather than a single clean letter, and it is preserved here exactly as printed without alteration. According to the course material on lower back and lower limb pain, the straight leg raising test is a standard clinical examination maneuver utilized in evaluating lumbar spine disorders. Specifically, the lecture lists a positive straight leg raising test under the clinical signs of radiculopathy and discusses it in the context of both lumbar disc prolapse and sciatica. When the lower limb is elevated with the knee extended, tension is placed on the lumbosacral nerve roots (particularly L5 and S1) and the sciatic nerve. If mechanical compression or inflammation—such as that caused by a posterolateral lumbar disc herniation—is present, stretching the compromised neural structures reproduces radicular pain and nerve root tension signs, yielding a positive test.\n\nBecause a positive straight leg raising test indicates mechanical irritation of the nerve roots exiting the lumbar spine (radiculopathy, option A) as well as pain referred along the course of the sciatic nerve (sciatica, option C, which is itself most commonly caused by L5 or S1 root compromise from disc prolapse), both conditions are valid clinical interpretations in the course framework.\n- Cauda equina syndrome (option B) can present with bilateral positive straight leg raising tests due to extensive root compression, but the test is primarily taught as a classic sign of radiculopathy and sciatica rather than being the defining diagnostic hallmark of cauda equina syndrome. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Recognize that straight leg raising test indicates radiculopathy and sciatica.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q332, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-333', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'What is the main treatment goal for a patient diagnosed with cauda equina syndrome?',
  options:['Pain management with muscle relaxants', 'Immediate surgical decompression', 'Physical therapy', 'Conservative rest and observation'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'Immediate surgical decompression is the keyed answer. According to the course material on cauda equina syndrome, this condition occurs due to a large central lumbar disc herniation compressing the cauda equina and requires early surgical intervention as any delay leads to permanent neurological deficit. Clinically, cauda equina syndrome manifests with low back pain, bilateral sciatica, lower motor neuron sphincteric disturbances (urine retention with overflow incontinence and chronic constipation), autonomic sexual dysfunction, bilateral lower motor neuron motor weakness ("flail foot"), bilateral sensory hypesthesia, saddle-shaped hypesthesia due to sacral root affection, and absent ankle and knee reflexes bilaterally. Because unremitting compression of the cauda equina roots causes ischemia and axonal destruction, prompt surgical decompression is an absolute medical and surgical emergency.\n\nThe distractor options represent inappropriate or dangerous management approaches:\n- Pain management with muscle relaxants is incorrect because pharmacological therapy fails to relieve mechanical root compression and arrest progressive neurological decline.\n- Physical therapy is incorrect and contraindicated in an acute surgical emergency requiring immediate neural decompression.\n- Conservative rest and observation is extremely dangerous and wrong, as any delay in surgical decompression for cauda equina syndrome leads to permanent, irreversible neurological deficits including permanent sphincteric and sexual dysfunction. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify immediate surgical decompression as the treatment goal for cauda equina syndrome.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q333, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-334', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'What is the first-line investigation for a patient with uncomplicated mechanical low back pain?',
  options:['MRI of the lumbar spine', 'X-ray of the lumbosacral spine', 'No imaging required', 'CBC, ESR, and markers'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'No imaging required is the keyed answer. According to the lecture investigations section, it is recommended that no laboratory investigations be ordered for patients with uncomplicated mechanical low back pain. Furthermore, no imaging is recommended for acute mechanical low back pain in the absence of clinical red flags. Patients with uncomplicated acute low back pain and no red flags, who are between 20 and 50 years old, do not require imaging. The course material emphasizes that while imaging can help confirm a clinical diagnosis, it cannot confirm that a particular anatomical structure is the direct cause of a patient\'s pain, making routine investigation unnecessary for self-limiting mechanical back pain.\n\nThe distractor options represent unnecessary over-investigation:\n- MRI of the lumbar spine is incorrect as a first-line test for uncomplicated acute mechanical low back pain without red flags or radicular symptoms.\n- X-ray of the lumbosacral spine is incorrect for acute uncomplicated mechanical low back pain without red flags (although plain X-rays may be appropriate in chronic low back pain per L11, they are not indicated as first-line tests for acute uncomplicated cases).\n- CBC, ESR, and markers are incorrect because routine laboratory investigations are not recommended unless there is clinical suspicion of a systemic cause such as inflammatory arthritis, connective tissue disease, or infection. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify that no imaging is required for uncomplicated mechanical low back pain.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q334, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-335', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'In which of the following scenarios should you order imaging for low back pain?',
  options:['Acute LBP without red flags in a 35-year-old', 'Chronic low back pain in a 55-year-old with no neurological symptoms', 'Low back pain radiating below the knees in a 45-year-old', 'Mechanical low back pain with no associated symptoms'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'Low back pain radiating below the knees in a 45-year-old is the keyed scenario. The lecture on investigations explicitly states that if the pain radiates below the knees, an MRI may be indicated. Radiation below the knee serves as a clinical indicator of lumbar radiculopathy resulting from nerve root compression (such as lumbar disc prolapse or sciatica). Conversely, the course material establishes that patients with uncomplicated acute low back pain and no red flags, who are between 20 and 50 years old, do not require imaging. Crucially, while L11 explicitly states that in chronic low back pain it may be appropriate to take AP and lateral lumbosacral X-rays (showing that plain X-rays have a recognized role in chronic LBP on its own), below-knee radiation specifically triggers the radicular imaging indication where an advanced MRI is warranted to visualize soft tissue neural compression. Thus, below-knee radiation represents the precise clinical scenario requiring targeted imaging among the choices.\n\nThe distractor options fail to meet the specific imaging criteria:\n- Acute LBP without red flags in a 35-year-old does not require imaging per L11 guidelines for patients between 20 and 50 years old.\n- Chronic low back pain in a 55-year-old with no neurological symptoms is incorrect as the primary trigger for advanced radicular MRI compared to below-knee radiation, even though L11 notes that plain X-rays may be appropriate in chronic LBP.\n- Mechanical low back pain with no associated symptoms requires conservative management and no imaging. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Identify that low back pain radiating below the knees indicates imaging.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q335, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-336', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A patient present with sensory loss bilaterally below the level of the umbilicus. Which spinal level is most likely involved?',
  options:['T5', 'T8', 'T10', 'L1'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'Explanation: T10 is the spinal level that corresponds to the umbilicus. T10 is the keyed spinal level. Sensory loss bilaterally below the level of the umbilicus points directly to a spinal cord lesion or sensory level at the T10 dermatome, which anatomically corresponds to the umbilicus as stated in the printed box and anatomical teaching.\n\nThe distractor spinal levels are incorrect:\n- T5 is incorrect because T4 (not T5) corresponds to the nipple line landmark [not taken from the course material], placing T5 slightly inferior to the nipple level.\n- T8 is incorrect because T8 corresponds to the mid-thoracic region located between the nipple line and the umbilicus [not taken from the course material].\n- L1 is incorrect because L1 corresponds to the inguinal ligament region [not taken from the course material], situated significantly inferior to the umbilicus.',
  objective:'Associate bilateral sensory loss below the umbilicus with the T10 spinal level.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q336, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-337', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A 45-year-old patient presents with sensory loss along the medial shin and front of the knee. On examination, weakness is noted in the hamstrings, and tibialis anterior muscles. Which spinal level is most likely affected?',
  options:['L3', 'L4', 'L5', 'S1'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'L4 is the keyed spinal level. According to the course material on lumbar disc prolapse and nerve root affection, herniation causes mechanical compression of exiting nerve roots, with the L4 root affected in L3-L4 disc herniation. Clinically, compression of the L4 nerve root results in motor weakness in the tibialis anterior and hamstring muscles alongside sensory changes along the medial shin and front of the knee [not taken from the course material]. This specific constellation of motor and sensory findings maps precisely to the L4 myotome and dermatome.\n\nThe distractor spinal levels do not fit this clinical presentation:\n- L3 is incorrect because L3 radiculopathy primarily affects the anterior thigh sensory distribution and quadriceps motor function [not taken from the course material], rather than causing sensory loss on the medial shin and weakness of the tibialis anterior and hamstrings.\n- L5 is incorrect because L5 radiculopathy (commonly affected in L4-L5 disc herniation) classically causes numbness in the outer thigh, leg, and dorsum of the foot, along with weakness of foot and great toe dorsiflexion [not taken from the course material], distinct from medial shin sensory loss and hamstring/tibialis anterior weakness.\n- S1 is incorrect because S1 radiculopathy (commonly affected in L5-S1 disc herniation) presents with sciatica radiating down the back of the leg to the foot, sensory changes over the posterior calf and sole, and plantar flexion weakness with absent ankle reflexes [not taken from the course material], differing from L4 clinical features. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate medial shin sensory loss and tibialis anterior weakness with the L4 spinal level.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q337, book p.63'
},

{
  id:'npqb-nr-338', bank:'gradegain', module:'neuropsych', chapter:'nr-backpain',
  stem:'A 50-year-old patient complains of difficulty extending their great toe, and they have sensory loss on the dorsum of the foot. The affected spinal level is:',
  options:['L3', 'L4', 'L5', 'S1'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'L5 is the keyed spinal level. According to the course material, lumbar disc prolapse occurs when the nucleus pulposus herniates through a tear in the annulus fibrosus, with the L5 root commonly affected in L4-L5 disc herniation. The lecture details that radiculopathy produces motor weakness in muscles supplied by the compressed root and dermatomal sensory changes. Clinically, compression of the L5 nerve root results in motor weakness of foot and great toe dorsiflexion (such as extensor hallucis longus weakness) and sensory loss or paresthesia over the dorsum of the foot and outer leg [not taken from the course material], perfectly matching this vignette.\n\nThe distractor spinal levels do not fit this clinical presentation:\n- L3 is incorrect because L3 radiculopathy involves the anterior thigh and quadriceps [not taken from the course material], not great toe extension or dorsum of foot sensation.\n- L4 is incorrect because L4 radiculopathy causes sensory loss along the medial shin and front of the knee with weakness of the tibialis anterior and hamstrings [not taken from the course material], distinct from L5 great-toe extension weakness and dorsum-of-foot numbness.\n- S1 is incorrect because S1 radiculopathy (commonly affected in L5-S1 disc herniation) presents with pain radiating down the posterior aspect of the leg to the foot, sensory changes over the posterior calf and sole, and plantar flexion weakness with absent ankle reflexes [not taken from the course material], rather than great toe extension weakness. Written for this bank — neuropsychiatry & neurosurgery qb.pdf prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Associate great-toe extension weakness and dorsum of foot sensory loss with the L5 spinal level.',
  source:'Grade Gain neuropsychiatry &amp; neurosurgery qb.pdf, Neurology topic 14 "Back &amp; Lower Limb Pain," Q338, book p.63'
}
